 "bloques": [
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "I like it.",
   "texto": [
    "Unlike Spanish, in French the person who likes something is the subject, as in English: **J'aime le café** (I like coffee).",
    "**J'adore** = I love it. **Je n'aime pas** = I don't like it. **Je déteste** = hate.",
    "When the complement is a noun that expresses a general taste, **le, la, les**: **J'aime le poisson**, **Je déteste les œufs** is used."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Undefined individuals and plural",
   "texto": [
    "For a non-accurate amount of a non-accounting food, the **du, de la, de l'**: **du** pain (masculine), **de la** viande (feminine), **de l'**eau (before vocal) partitions are used.",
    "For several unspecified elements the plural article **des**: **des fruits** is used. In Spanish, these articles are often omitted.",
    "Compare: **J'aime le café** (the coffee in general) and **Je prends du café** (I drink coffee: a quantity)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Articles in negative",
   "texto": [
    "In a simple denial, partitives and indefinite plural usually move to **de**: **Je ne prends pas de café**, **Je ne mange pas de fruits**.",
    "In some contrasts and with **être** the partitive: **Ce n'est pas du café, c'est du thé** is retained.",
    "With **aimer**, the defined article: **Je n'aime pas le café** is maintained."
   ]
  },
